      <p class="lead"><strong>Cold plunge chillers split three ways: by system form, by how much plumbing you take on, and by cooling technology. System form is the split that matters most when you're buying.</strong></p>

      <p>I dug through the buyer's guides and manufacturer pages to sort out what &ldquo;types of chillers&rdquo; actually means, because most sites use the word loosely. Here's the breakdown that actually helps you choose.</p>

      <h2 id="system-form">By system form</h2>
      <p class="sub">The split that matters most when you're buying.</p>

      <h3>Integrated tub-and-chiller combos</h3>
      <p>The tub, chiller, plumbing, and filtration all come as one product. You place it, fill it, plug it in, and set a temperature.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Easiest to install and live with</li>
        <li>Usually quiet and tuned for 38&ndash;55&deg;F operation</li>
        <li>Most expensive option, and harder to swap or upgrade just the chiller</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>External inline chillers</h3>
      <p>A separate refrigeration box that plumbs into any vessel you already have. A stock tank, a barrel, a standalone tub.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A pump pulls water from the tub through the chiller and back</li>
        <li>Very flexible, works with almost any tub shape or material</li>
        <li>You can upgrade the chiller without replacing the tub</li>
        <li>More plumbing, and more points of failure if the build is sloppy</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Portable or drop-in chillers</h3>
      <p>Small, rim-mounted units with a coil or element that hangs directly in the water. No external plumbing.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Easiest to install and move</li>
        <li>Lower upfront cost</li>
        <li>Usually can't hold a full-size tub below 50&ndash;55&deg;F reliably, and don't hold up well to daily use</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="plumbing">By plumbing complexity</h2>
      <p class="sub">How much of the build you take on yourself.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bare standalone chillers:</strong> just the cooling unit &mdash; you supply the pump, hoses, and filter. Cheapest per unit of cooling power, most complex to assemble.</li>
        <li><strong>All-in-one cabinets:</strong> chiller, pump, and filtration in one box, often with UV or ozone sanitation built in. Easier setup than a bare chiller, more expensive.</li>
        <li><strong>Fully integrated systems:</strong> same as the tub-and-chiller combo above &mdash; the manufacturer controls everything from tub shape to plumbing to app integration.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="cooling-method">By cooling method</h2>
      <p class="sub">How the unit actually removes heat.</p>

      <h3>Air-cooled vs. water-cooled</h3>
      <p>Air-cooled units use ambient air and a fan to dump heat. This is the standard for home setups &mdash; no cooling tower needed. Water-cooled units use a water loop, typically a cooling tower or building condenser loop, and show up mostly in commercial or spa installs.</p>

      <h3>Recirculating vs. immersion</h3>
      <p>Immersion chillers drop a cold coil directly into the tub water. Lower cost, easier setup, slower, and better suited to light use. Recirculating chillers sit outside the tub with an internal heat exchanger and a pump moving water out and back. Faster cooling, better temperature stability, and this is what most serious home and commercial setups use.</p>

      <h2 id="capacity">By capacity</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>1/4 to 1/3 HP:</strong> small tubs under 100 gallons, indoor, mild climates. Can reach very cold temps but take longer to cool initially.</li>
        <li><strong>1/2 HP:</strong> the sweet spot for most home setups, 100&ndash;150 gallons or warmer climates, faster cooldown and recovery between plunges.</li>
        <li><strong>1 HP and up:</strong> larger tubs, outdoor use in hot climates, or back-to-back use by multiple people. Commercial systems scale up to multiple tons for rehab centers and sports facilities.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="cool-heat">Cool-only vs. cool-and-heat</h2>
      <p class="sub">Do you need heat too, or just cold?</p>

      <p>Some chillers only cool, holding water in the 38&ndash;55&deg;F range. Others are dual-mode heat pumps that cool down to 32&ndash;38&deg;F and heat up to 100&ndash;113&deg;F from the same unit, which is what lets you run contrast therapy out of a single tub. Dual-mode units are increasingly common at the higher end of both residential and commercial lines.</p>

      <h2 id="home-commercial">Home vs. commercial</h2>
      <p class="sub">Scale and duty cycle.</p>

      <p>Home and general-purpose chillers run 0.3 to 1 HP, air-cooled, built for one or two users and 80&ndash;200 gallons of daily use. High-capacity outdoor or commercial chillers run 1&ndash;2 HP at the consumer scale, or multiple tons commercially, and are built for continuous duty with heavier filtration and sanitation.</p>

      <h2 id="putting-together">Putting it together</h2>
      <p class="sub">Four questions to map yourself to a type.</p>

      <p>If you're just trying to map yourself to a type, it comes down to four questions. What form do you want (integrated, external, or portable)? How much complexity are you willing to take on (bare chiller, all-in-one cabinet, or fully integrated)? What scale do you need (1/4 HP up to multi-ton)? And do you need heat too, or just cold?</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>What's the difference between a chiller and a cold plunge tub?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>The tub holds the water. The chiller cools it. Some products bundle both into one unit, others sell them separately so you can pair any tub with any compatible chiller.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq">
        <summary>Do I need a recirculating chiller?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>Not always. If you're plunging occasionally in a small tub, an immersion or drop-in chiller can work. If you're plunging daily, sharing the tub with others, or want stable temperature recovery between sessions, a recirculating chiller is the better fit.</p></div>
      </details>

      <p>Once you've picked a type, reliability matters too &mdash; see our <a href="/learn/cold-plunge-chiller-reliability-index">Cold Plunge Chiller Reliability Index</a> for what owner and reviewer reports actually say about failure patterns.</p>

